  m_text: Bohr's atomic model explains that an atom has a small, positively charged
    nucleus at its center, with negatively charged electrons moving around it in
    fixed energy levels or shells. Each shell has a specific energy, and electrons
    can move from one level to another by absorbing or releasing energy. The model
    helped explain atomic stability and the emission of light during electron transitions.
